    ; ── the second grammar (parser slice 19) ────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ parseJSONText. A `.json` file is not parsed by the routine above with a
    ; flag set — it gets its OWN entry point, a different grammar, reached from
    ; ParseSourceFile before parseSourceFileWorker is ever called. Reading the two
    ; side by side is the whole slice: this one has no statement list, no
    ; declarations and no recovery loop; what it has is a single VALUE, wrapped
    ; in an ExpressionStatement so the SourceFile shape stays the same.
    ;
    ; The value's grammar is the ordinary JavaScript one — parse_object_literal_
    ; expression and parse_array_literal_expression are the same routines an
    ; expression uses — and JSON-ness is enforced AFTERWARDS, by a validating
    ; walk over the finished tree (validate_json_value). So `{"a": a + 1}` parses
    ; into a real BinaryExpression and is then reported as error 1328. That is
    ; not an economy of the reference's: it is what lets the JSON parse produce a
    ; TREE for a tsconfig with a comment, a trailing comma or a single-quoted
    ; key, which is the whole point of the kind existing.
    ;
    ; ★ The ERROR RECOVERY arm is the one worth reading twice. Several top-level
    ; values in one file (`1 2 3`) are collected and wrapped in a SYNTHESIZED
    ; ArrayLiteralExpression that no bracket in the source produced, and only the
    ; FIRST of them reports `Unexpected_token` — reported inside the loop, before
    ; the second value is parsed, which is what puts it in front of anything that
    ; value reports.
    procedure parse_json_text(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked



















































































































































    ; validateJsonValue. Answers nothing: it either finds the value acceptable or
    ; appends a diagnostic, and the two recursive arms (object, array) do the
    ; same for what they contain.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's `break` — the PrefixUnaryExpression arm's escape when the
    ; operator is not a minus or the operand is not a number — leaves the switch
    ; and falls to the report at the bottom, which is why that arm is written
    ; here as "return when acceptable" rather than as "report when not".
    procedure validate_json_value(mutable this, value_expression: ref[AstNode]?) @deep true linked





































































    ; validateJsonObjectLiteral. Two complaints per property and they are
    ; independent: an element that is not a PropertyAssignment at all (a spread,
    ; a shorthand, a method) is reported and SKIPPED, while a property with a
    ; name that is not a double-quoted string is reported and its value still
    ; validated.
    procedure validate_json_object_literal(mutable this, n: ref[AstNode]) @deep true linked


































    ; getErrorSpanForNode + the append, which is where the JSON route differs
    ; from every other diagnostic in this port and the difference is deliberate.
    ;
    ; ★★★ A RAW APPEND. validateJsonValue writes `p.diagnostics = append(...)`
    ; directly rather than going through parseErrorAtRange, so it gets neither
    ; the DEDUP nor `hasParseError`. Both matter: a JSON complaint at the same
    ; position as the last parse error would be swallowed by the dedup, and the
    ; validation runs AFTER the SourceFile node is finished, so a has_error set
    ; here would be stamped on whatever node a later parse finishes — there is
    ; none, but the reference's shape is the one that stays right when that
    ; changes.
    ;
    ; ★ The POS is trivia-skipped and the END is not: a diagnostic aimed at a
    ; node points at its first real character, and a node's recorded pos is its
    ; full start.
    procedure report_json_error(mutable this, n: ref[AstNode], code: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true linked


    ; isDoubleQuotedString: a string literal whose SingleQuote flag is clear.
    ; Both halves are load-bearing — a non-string name (`{a: 1}`) is not a
    ; double-quoted string either, which is how an identifier key gets error
    ; 1327 — and the flag is the field slice 19 added to LiteralData for exactly
    ; this reader.
    function is_double_quoted_string(n: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked











    ; ★★★ reparseTopLevelAwait — the reference's SECOND PASS over the statement
    ; list, and the last construct in this port with a route behind it.
    ;
    ; The problem it solves: `await` is an identifier in a script and an operator
    ; in a module, and whether a file is a module is not known until the whole
    ; file has been read (an `export` anywhere decides it). So the reference
    ; parses once with AwaitContext off, notes which top-level statements used
    ; `await` as a name, and then — if the file turned out to be a module —
    ; re-parses exactly those statements with the flag on. `const foo = await {
    ; bar: 42 }` is an identifier followed by an object literal on the first pass
    ; and an AwaitExpression on the second: a different TREE, not a different
    ; flag, which is why the detection alone could never have matched.
    ;
    ; Three things travel together and each is easy to drop:
    ;
    ;   the STATEMENTS   copied through unchanged outside the spans, re-parsed
    ;                    inside them
    ;   the DIAGNOSTICS  re-sliced, not re-run: the ones reported over a copied
    ;                    range are copied with it, the ones over a re-parsed
    ;                    range are DISCARDED and replaced by whatever the second
    ;                    pass reports
    ;   the SCANNER      rewound after each span, so the file ends where it ended
    ;
    ; Answers the new statement list, or null if the reparse met something this
    ; port cannot handle (which the first pass, by definition, did not).
    procedure reparse_top_level_await(mutable this, source_statements: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]? linked










































































































    ; The reference's two FindIndex calls over the saved list, which pick the
    ; diagnostics belonging to a copied range: from the first whose position
    ; reaches `from_pos`, up to (excluding) the first at-or-after that one whose
    ; position reaches `to_pos`. With `bounded` false there is no upper bound and
    ; the rest of the list is copied.
    ;
    ; ★ Ported as a scan rather than as two searches because the list is in
    ; position order and short; what is NOT collapsed is the two-stage shape —
    ; the upper bound is sought FROM the lower one, so a diagnostic before
    ; `from_pos` can never end the range even if it is at-or-after `to_pos`.
    procedure copy_saved_diagnostics(mutable this, saved: ref[Array[Diagnostic]], from_pos: int, to_pos: int, bounded: bool) @deep true linked






























    ; ★★★ parseToplevelStatement — the RECORDING half of the top-level-await
    ; reparse, whose acting half is reparse_top_level_await above.
    ;
    ; Upstream parses the file ONCE, notes which top-level statements used
    ; `await` as an identifier while not in an await context, and then RE-PARSES
    ; exactly those statements with AwaitContext on (reparseTopLevelAwait) —
    ; re-positioning the scanner, re-slicing the diagnostics, and replacing the
    ; statements in place.
    ;
    ; ★ Slice 5 ported the DETECTION alone and reported the construct, on the
    ; argument that detection is what decides whether the tree this port produced
    ; is the tree the reference produced — when the answer is yes, every node of
    ; that statement carries AwaitContext upstream and none of them do here. That
    ; was right, and it is worth knowing how it was found: the difference DID
    ; surface as an unexplained failure on two corpus cases, the moment member
    ; access started parsing and `M.await(1)` became reachable. Slice 15 makes
    ; the record act instead of report.
    procedure parse_top_level_statement(mutable this) @page true @returnpage true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked


















































































    ; ★★★ The reparse runs only for an external MODULE:
    ;
    ;     !result.IsDeclarationFile && result.ExternalModuleIndicator != nil
    ;                                && len(p.possibleAwaitSpans) > 0
    ;
    ; and the indicator is `isAnExternalModuleIndicatorNode` over the top-level
    ; statements: an export modifier, an import declaration, an export
    ; assignment, an export declaration, an `import x = require(…)`, or an
    ; `import.meta` anywhere in the tree.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THIS FUNCTION IS WHERE SLICE 8 PAID §3.5l. Through slice 7 it tested
    ; the export MODIFIER and nothing else, and the comment standing here said
    ; so with a reason: "every one of those but the FIRST is a construct this
    ; slice reports as unported, so a file that parsed to the end can only be a
    ; module by the export-modifier arm". That reason was true and it EXPIRED
    ; the moment import and export declarations started parsing — three of the
    ; five remaining arms are exactly what this slice built. A condition whose
    ; correctness rests on another part of the port being incomplete has to be
    ; re-read by the slice that completes it.
    ;
    ; (`IsDeclarationFile` is the `.d.ts` name test; the pinned corpus has no
    ; such case, as the oracle's own header records.)
    ;
    ; The detection itself was NOT in the first version of this check, and the
    ; control that found it is worth keeping in mind: removing it altogether
    ; turned two corpus cases red and the fixture MATCHED — which can only mean
    ; the fixture was being reported for something the reference does not do. It
    ; had no export, so it was never a module.
    ; ★★★ AND SINCE SLICE 28 IT IS NOT THE WHOLE INDICATOR — see
    ; external_module_indicator below. `isFileProbablyExternalModule` is one arm
    ; of `getExternalModuleIndicator`, which the reference calls from
    ; finishSourceFile and stores on the file; every reader — the reparse
    ; condition here, and the binder's IsExternalModule — reads the STORED
    ; indicator and not this function. The difference is the JSON arm, and it
    ; became load-bearing when the binder started naming module symbols.
    function file_is_external_module(file: ref[AstNode], statements: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked
















    ; getExternalModuleIndicator, and it is the function every reader of "is this
    ; file a module" actually asks — the reference computes it once in
    ; finishSourceFile and stores the NODE on the SourceFile; this port answers
    ; the question instead of storing the node (§3.5q), so the arms are here.
    ;
    ;   1. a JSON file is NEVER an external module, whatever its statements say
    ;   2. isFileProbablyExternalModule (the function above)
    ;   3. a DECLARATION file stops here — the two arms below are for
    ;      implementation files only
    ;   4. opts.JSX: a JSX tag makes the file a module, under
    ;      jsx: react-jsx / react-jsxdev
    ;   5. opts.Force: under moduleDetection force/auto every file is one
    ;
    ; ★ Arms 4 and 5 are NOT ported and cannot be reached from anything this port
    ; has: both are decided by `ExternalModuleIndicatorOptions`, which
    ; GetExternalModuleIndicatorOptions derives from the COMPILER OPTIONS, and
    ; this port has no compiler options — the reference's own oracle builds
    ; SourceFileParseOptions with that struct zeroed, so both are false on the
    ; other side of every yardstick too. The slice that ports tsconfig owns them;
    ; until then arm 3 is unreachable as well, since with 4 and 5 off a
    ; declaration file's answer is `false` either way (which is why it is written
    ; as a comment rather than as an `if` that cannot change an answer).
    ;
    ; ★★ Arm 1 is the load-bearing one and it is why this function exists at all:
    ; JSON is the ONE script kind whose statements can satisfy arm 2 — a
    ; `package.json` body is an object literal, not an export — while the
    ; reference still refuses it modulehood, and the binder's JSON arm depends on
    ; the refusal. `IsJsonSourceFile` asks the file's SCRIPT KIND upstream; the
    ; equivalent here is the JsonFile context flag the parser stamps on every node
    ; of a JSON parse.
    ; ★`statements` is nullable because the SINK says so: file_is_external_module
    ; below opens with `if statements = null return false`, and a file with no
    ; statement list is not an external module. A mixed parameter list -- one
    ; nullable, one not, feeding a callee that guards both -- is the signpost.
    function external_module_indicator(file: ref[AstNode]?, statements: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked








    ; getImportMetaIfNecessary. The GUARD is the whole point of the reference's
    ; own comment on it: a full tree walk is what finding `import.meta` costs, so
    ; the flag exists to skip it, and the parser sets that flag exactly where it
    ; builds the node. Answering the question without the flag would be correct
    ; and would walk every tree; answering it with the flag and no node would be
    ; the flag lying, which is why it says POSSIBLY contains.
    function import_meta_if_necessary(file: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked








    ; findChildNode(root, IsImportMeta), which checks the ROOT first and then
    ; recurses through ForEachChild. The reference answers the NODE and this
    ; answers whether there is one, because the only caller asks `!= nil` — the
    ; indicator node itself is stored on the SourceFile upstream and nothing in
    ; this port reads it (§3.5q: carry the ANSWER, not the value, where the value
    ; is asked one question).
    function find_import_meta(n: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked

















    ; IsImportMeta: a MetaProperty built from the `import` keyword whose name is
    ; literally `meta`. BOTH halves need a field this port added for them —
    ; the keyword token (slice 18) and the identifier's text (slice 17) — which
    ; is why the arm could not be written earlier even though the walk could:
    ; without the keyword `new.target` answers yes, and without the text
    ; `import.anything` does.
    function is_import_meta(n: ref[AstNode]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked


















    ; isAnExternalModuleIndicatorNode, arm for arm — the five STATEMENT arms.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's indicator has a sixth arm which is not a statement test at
    ; all: `import.meta` ANYWHERE in the tree, found by a walk under the
    ; PossiblyContainsImportMeta flag. It stood here as absent through four
    ; slices, with a claim dated to "the slice that ports the import EXPRESSION",
    ; and slice 18 is that slice — it lives at import_meta_if_necessary, called
    ; from file_is_external_module after this loop, exactly where
    ; isFileProbablyExternalModule calls it.
    function statement_is_external_module_indicator(n: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked


















    ; ast.HasSyntacticModifier(node, ModifierFlagsExport), asked of the node's
    ; own children: a modifier is an ordinary Token node here, and the three
    ; declaration forms this slice builds all carry theirs as leading children.
    function statement_has_export_modifier(n: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked














    ; ── statements ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference's parseStatement switch, arm for arm. The arms this slice
    ; does not implement are named individually rather than caught by a default,
    ; so that adding one is a matter of removing it from the list below.

    procedure parse_statement(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked
















































































































    ; The keywords that route to parseDeclaration when isStartOfDeclaration says
