    ; identifier, so the look-ahead decides.
    ;
    ; It is a table rather than a run of `if`s in parse_statement for the §3.9b
    ; reason — eighteen arms sharing ONE consequent, where the arms above each
    ; have their own. Its twin, `statement_start_is_unported`, is gone with
    ; slice 9: every token that was on it now has a destination.
    function statement_start_is_declaration_keyword(k: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked








































    ; ── the declaration router ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Every declaration form goes through here, because every one of them can
    ; carry modifiers and the modifiers are parsed before anyone knows what they
    ; modify. Only the variable-statement arm is ported; the rest report
    ; themselves, so this slice buys the ROUTE rather than the destinations —
    ; which is the point, since the route is what the 49 % of corpus cases
    ; blocked on `export`, `const` and `declare` were stopped by.

    procedure parse_declaration(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked






























    ; The reference keeps a ModifierList carrying a precomputed ModifierFlags
    ; bit set; here the list is a plain Array of Token nodes and the question is
    ; asked of it directly. Same answer — ModifiersToFlags is a fold over exactly
    ; these kinds — and it keeps the port from needing a second flag table whose
    ; only job is to mirror the Kind table.
    function has_modifier(modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?, kind: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked















    procedure parse_declaration_worker(mutable this, pos: int, jsdoc: int, modifiers: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) @page true @direct true @deep true @deep_this_8 true @deep_whole_8 true returns ref[AstNode]? linked













































































    ; ════ MODIFIERS AND DECORATORS (slices 2 and 12) ════════════════════════
    ;
    ; parseModifiersEx. Answers the list, or null when there is none — the
    ; reference returns a nil ModifierList in that case and ForEachChild then
    ; visits no modifier at all, which is what keeps `var x;` a one-child
    ; statement.
    ;
    ; ── the DECORATOR, slice 12 ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The last construct the unported histogram named: ten corpus cases, all
    ; ten bought. It is four small pieces and none of them is a route —
    ; parse_decorator and parse_decorator_expression below, the two-run
    ; bookkeeping in the loop, and parse_decorated_expression down at the class
    ; family (the `@` arm of the primary expression).
    ;
    ; ★ Four call sites pass allow_decorators = true, which is the reference's
    ; count exactly: the declaration router, the class member, the parameter and
    ; the object-literal element. The FOURTH is dead in the reference too and is
    ; ported as written rather than dropped — isListElement(PCObjectLiteralMembers)
    ; admits `[`, `*`, `...`, `.` and a literal property name, and `@` is none
    ; of them, so the literal ends before parseObjectLiteralElement is ever
    ; asked. A control that turns that one to false changes nothing, and the
    ; reason it changes nothing is a fact about the reference (§3.5v, the
    ; "unreachable" row), not a gap in the fixtures.

    ; ★ A DECORATOR IS AN ELEMENT OF THIS LIST, not a slot of its own on the
    ; node that carries it. That is the reference's shape — one ModifierList
    ; holding both, with `Node.Decorators()` a FILTER over it — and it is why
    ; slice 12 added no field to any carrying record.
    ;
    ; The three booleans are the reference's, and they encode one rule the
    ; comment there states and the code enforces: decorators may appear in TWO
    ; runs (`[...leadingDecorators, ...leadingModifiers, ...trailingDecorators,
    ; ...trailingModifiers]`) but not in three. Once a modifier has followed a
    ; TRAILING decorator, `hasTrailingModifier` shuts the decorator arm off for
    ; good, and the loop ends at the next `@` because try_parse_modifier answers
    ; nothing for it. Having both runs at once is illegal and is left to the
    ; CHECKER — the parser builds the list and says nothing, so `export @dec
    ; class C {}` parses here exactly as it does upstream.
    procedure parse_modifiers(mutable this, allow_decorators: bool, permit_const_as_modifier: bool, stop_on_start_of_class_static_block: bool) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]? linked




















































    ; parseDecorator. The `@` is consumed, then a LEFT-HAND-SIDE expression —
    ; not an assignment expression: `@a.b.c()` is the whole of what a decorator
    ; may be, which is what keeps `@a = b` from parsing.
    ;
    ; ★ The context flag is restored BEFORE finish_node, because doInContext
    ; returns before the node is built. So the Decorator node itself does NOT
    ; carry DecoratorContext while every node of its expression does — a
    ; one-line ordering the yardstick can see, and a control is aimed at it.
    procedure parse_decorator(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked














    ; parseDecoratorExpression. `@await` inside an [Await] context is disallowed
    ; and, in the reference's words, "can cause parsing to go off the rails" — so
    ; it reports Expression_expected, takes the `await` as the identifier it
    ; cannot be, steps over it and carries on through the member and call rests.
    ; That recovery is why the arm exists at all, and it is only portable now.
    procedure parse_decorator_expression(mutable this) @page true @returnpage true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked



















