    ; ★★★ Three things landed here with slice 13c, and the hand-written loop this
    ; replaces had all three folded into "stop and report".
    ;
    ; (a) The statements are a LIST — parse_list(PCBlockStatements) — so the
    ;     block joins `parsing_contexts` and a broken statement inside it can
    ;     ask whether the block, or anything outside it, could use the token.
    ; (b) `ignore_missing_open_brace` parses the body EVEN WITH NO `{`, which is
    ;     the arrow-function recovery ParseFlagsIgnoreMissingOpenBrace exists
    ;     for; without the open brace and without the flag the reference builds a
    ;     Block over a MISSING list, which is an empty one here.
    ; (c) The `=` arm, which is a diagnostic about what the user probably meant:
    ;     a block followed by `=` is most likely a destructuring assignment that
    ;     needed parentheses. It reports and CONSUMES the `=`, so the enclosing
    ;     statement list does not see it and report again.
    procedure parse_block_ex(mutable this, ignore_missing_open_brace: bool, code: int) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked




































    ; ── the two statements a function BODY needs ────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; They land with slice 4 rather than with the statement spine because that is
    ; when they became reachable: before a function declaration parsed, no
    ; `return` in the corpus was ever looked at, and the unported histogram said
    ; so — `return` and `if` were absent from it entirely and went straight to
    ; third and fourth place the moment function bodies started parsing.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's parseExpectedMatchingBrackets(`(`, `)`, …) is a
    ; parse_expected here: its whole extra job is to attach a "the parser
    ; expected to find a `)` to match the `(` here" note to a diagnostic, and on
    ; the path where the bracket IS there the two are the same call.

    procedure parse_if_statement(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked



























