    ; ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    ; THE LOOP AND BRANCH STATEMENTS — parser slice 9
    ; ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    ;
    ; The remaining arms of parseStatement, which the slice-8 histogram named as
    ; 20.7 % of corpus first blockers across six tags. Unlike every slice since
    ; the second, these are SIX independent ports rather than one route — nothing
    ; here is shared machinery, which is why the row looked bigger than it is.
    ;
    ; ★ What they DO share is the `for` head, and it is the reason this row was
    ; taken before the three smaller ones: DisallowInContext is set nowhere else
    ; in the language, so until the for statement parsed, three claims in this
    ; port rested on its absence — parse_variable_declaration_list's two
    ; (see there) and parse_list_element's note that the difference between the
    ; array-literal and argument-expression element contexts "only shows inside a
    ; `for` initializer or a decorator, and both of those are unported, so no
    ; case in this suite can see it". §3.5p: a claim resting on another part of
    ; the port being incomplete has an expiry date, and this slice owns all
    ; three. It also makes slice 7's one UNREACHABLE control reachable — the enum
    ; member's DisallowIn clear — which is what parser_for_disallow_in_enum.ts
    ; is for.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's parseExpectedMatchingBrackets(`(`, `)`, …) is a
    ; parse_expected here, for the reason parse_if_statement already records: its
    ; whole extra job is to attach a "the parser expected to find a `)` to match
    ; the `(` here" note to a diagnostic, and on the path where the bracket IS
    ; there the two calls are the same.

    ; ★ statement BEFORE expression, both in the parse and in the walk: `do`
    ; runs its body before it evaluates its condition. See DoStatementData.
    procedure parse_do_statement(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked



























    procedure parse_while_statement(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked


















    ; ★★★ parseForOrForInOrForOfStatement — three statement kinds out of one
    ; head, and the SELECTION is a Go `switch` over boolean cases whose
    ; short-circuiting is load-bearing:
    ;
    ;     case awaitToken != nil && p.parseExpected(Of) || awaitToken == nil && p.parseOptional(Of):
    ;
    ; With an `await` present the `of` is REQUIRED — parseExpected reports a
    ; diagnostic when it is missing AND ANSWERS FALSE, so the head falls through
    ; to `in` and then to the three-part form — and without one it is merely
    ; optional, which lets the same head fall through without a word.
    procedure parse_for_or_for_in_or_for_of_statement(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked






























































































































