    ; automatic semicolon insertion.
    ;
    ; ★★★ parseSemicolon's RESULT is discarded at every one of the reference's
    ; fifteen call sites, and reading it is how slice 13c broke five corpus units
    ; before a single control was written: seventeen callers here said
    ; `if this.parse_semicolon() = false  return null`, which was right while the
    ; fallback stopped the port and became "answer no tree, report nothing" the
    ; moment it started reporting instead. The dumper prints that as
    ; `UNPORTED 0 no-tree 0` — an honest-looking report of a construct nobody
    ; built, for a defect in this port. **A guard that reads the result of
    ; something that has stopped failing is invisible in both directions.**
    ;
    ; The two halves are split apart since slice 4, because a caller needs the
    ; QUESTION on its own: a body-less overload signature is exactly
    ; "a function head that can_parse_semicolon()".
    function can_parse_semicolon(this) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns bool linked










    ; tryParseSemicolon. Split out of parse_semicolon with slice 6, because the
    ; property declaration needs the try WITHOUT the report: reaching the end of
    ; a property with no semicolon is one of four diagnostics there, and which
    ; one depends on what preceded it.
    procedure try_parse_semicolon(mutable this) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked









