    ; The other direction, and the ladder's: the scan never merges `>`, so
    ; parseBinaryExpressionRest asks for `>=`, `>>`, `>>>`, `>>=` and `>>>=`
    ; before EVERY operator test. See the scanner's own comment for why the
    ; asymmetry is deliberate.
    procedure re_scan_greater_than_token(mutable this) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns int linked





    ; The fourth, slice 16's: a `/` is a division sign until the parser says it
    ; is standing where an expression begins. The scanner has the whole account;
    ; what belongs here is that this wrapper needs neither of the two things its
    ; template sibling below carries — the scanner reports the one diagnostic it
    ; can produce (an unterminated literal) through the shared list since 13b,
    ; and nothing in the re-scan is unported, so there is no scanner record to
    ; raise.
    ; The file's TEXT, for the CHECKER's own scanner — the reference reads
    ; `sourceFile.Text()` off the node, where this port keeps it on the scanner
    ; the parse used. Its sibling, the language VARIANT, needs no accessor: the
    ; parser already carries it as a field and the checker reads it as one.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND A METHOD OF THAT NAME IS WHAT THIS SLICE TRIED FIRST — root
    ; CLAUDE.md's field-shadows-method trap, whose recorded symptom is *rc 0, the
    ; argument dropped, the field's value returned*. With a ZERO-ARGUMENT method
    ; it is LOUD instead, and the message names neither the field nor the method:
    ; `p.language_variant_jsx` resolves to the field, the `()` is left over as an
    ; orphan operand, and collapse_operand_sequence reports *unexpected operand —
    ; missing operator, or a ':'/newline separator* at the opening parenthesis.
    function source_text(this) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns Slice[char] linked


    procedure re_scan_slash_token(mutable this) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns int linked








    ; The third one, slice 11's: a `}` that closes a substitution is the first
    ; character of the template's next chunk, and only the parser knows that.
    ;
    ; ★★★ It carries a check its two siblings do not need, and leaving it out is
    ; a silent wrong tree rather than a missing report. next_token_without_check
    ; is where this port turns a scanner fault into an unported record — and a
    ; re-scan does not go through it, because it re-reads a token that has
    ; already been scanned. So `` `a${b}\01` `` and `` `a${b}c `` would come out
    ; of the re-scan carrying ContainsInvalidEscape and Unterminated, the
    ; reference would emit a diagnostic for each, and this port would answer a
    ; complete tree with nothing on it. The same test, applied at the same
    ; moment, is what keeps the stand-in consistent.
