    ; ── the recovery proper ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; abortParsingListOrMoveToNextToken. The list is looking at a token that
    ; starts no element of its own kind: report what THIS list wanted, then ask
    ; whether any ENCLOSING list could use the token. If one could, give up so
    ; that it gets it; if none could, the token belongs to nobody, so skip it and
    ; let this list try again.
    ;
    ; ★ Answering "should I abort" rather than "did I abort" is the reference's
    ; own shape and it is the load-bearing half: a `}` inside a broken class body
    ; must close the class rather than be skipped, and the only thing that knows
    ; is the enclosing list.
    procedure abort_parsing_list_or_move_to_next_token(mutable this, ctx: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked








    ; isInSomeParsingContext — true if the current token is an element or a
    ; terminator of ANY open list.
    ;
    ; ★ The element question is asked with in_error_recovery TRUE, which is the
    ; whole reason is_list_element takes that parameter: three of its arms
    ; tighten up under it (see there). The reference asserts that at least one
    ; context is open; here the source file's own list is opened first and is
    ; never closed while parsing, so the set is never empty either.
    procedure is_in_some_parsing_context(mutable this) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked
















    ; parsingContextErrors — what each list says when it cannot use the token it
    ; is looking at. Eighteen arms, one per context this port opens; the
    ; reference's remaining six belong to the JSX and JSDoc lists it does not.
    ;
    ; ★ Two arms ask a question about the TOKEN rather than about the list, and
    ; both are the same question: a KEYWORD in a name position gets a message
    ; naming it (`'0' is not allowed as a variable declaration name` / `… as a
    ; parameter name`), anything else gets the generic one. The message argument
    ; is the keyword's spelling, which is not compared; the CODE is.
