    procedure cparts_remove_leading_newlines(this, a: ref[Array[int]], first: int, count: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked




















    ; ── comment ranges ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; The reference's scanner.GetLeadingCommentRanges / GetTrailingCommentRanges,
    ; which it exposes as an ITERATOR (`iter.Seq[ast.CommentRange]`) and consumes
    ; in a `for … range`. Here the ranges are appended to a caller-supplied array
    ; instead: an iterator in Go is a closure, and Scaly's only escaping-closure
    ; form is `return \x: …` (§3.9d, the same substitution ForEachChild took).
    ;
    ; ★ The reference's CommentRange carries a KIND and a `HasTrailingNewLine`
    ; flag besides the range. Neither is stored, because the only consumer this
    ; port has is get_jsdoc_comment_ranges, whose filter reads Pos and End alone
    ; — a §3.5ao omission with its reader named: the flag's readers are the
    ; emitter's comment writer and the language service's formatter.
    ;
    ; ★★ `collecting` starts FALSE for a leading scan and true for a trailing
    ; one, and that is the whole difference between them: a trailing comment is
    ; one that follows a token on the SAME LINE, so the scan stops at the first
    ; line break; a leading scan skips whatever precedes the first line break and
    ; only starts collecting after it. Both stop at the first character that is
    ; neither whitespace nor a comment.
    procedure collect_comment_ranges(this, from: int, trailing: bool, mutable out: ref[Array[int]]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true linked











































































































































    ; The reference's GetJSDocCommentRanges. Seven node kinds take their JSDoc
    ; from the TRAILING comments of the position before them as well as from the
    ; leading ones — a parameter documented as `/** the x */ x` is the shape —
    ; and the trailing scan comes FIRST, which is the order the ranges end up in.
    ;
    ; The filter is the reference's, and each clause is a real case: a comment
    ; that reaches past the node's own end is not its documentation, `/**/` is
    ; not JSDoc (four characters, and the fourth must not be `/`), and a comment
    ; must open with `/**`.
    procedure get_jsdoc_comment_ranges(mutable this, n: ref[AstNode]?, mutable out: ref[Array[int]]) @deep true linked















































    ; ── the comment pragmas ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ★★★ getCommentPragmas + extractPragmas + processPragmasIntoFields, and the
    ; only thing a parser yardstick can see of them is TWO DIAGNOSTICS: TS1084
    ; for a triple-slash `<reference>` whose arguments name none of types/lib/
    ; path/no-default-lib, and TS1453 for a `resolution-mode` that is neither
    ; `require` nor `import`. Everything else a pragma does is a FIELD on the
    ; SourceFile — ReferencedFiles, TypeReferenceDirectives, LibReferenceDirectives,
    ; CheckJsDirective — whose readers are the program builder (which files to
    ; pull in), the emitter and the checker, none of which this port has.
    ;
    ; ★ Stated omissions, each with its reader, per §3.5ao:
    ;   · the three FileReference lists and CheckJsDirective are not STORED. They
    ;     are computed exactly far enough to decide which arm of the switch a
    ;     pragma takes, because that decision is what produces the diagnostics.
    ;   · the MULTI-LINE arm of extractPragmas — `@jsx`, `@jsxfrag`,
    ;     `@jsximportsource`, `@jsxruntime` inside a `/* */` — is not ported. It
    ;     produces no diagnostic in any input and its only readers are the
    ;     emitter's JSX factory selection and the checker's jsxImportSource, so
    ;     porting it would be forty lines nothing in this repository can measure
    ;     in either direction.
    ;   · an argument whose name is none of the six the switch reads is not kept.
    ;     The reference puts it in the map and never looks it up, and the default
    ;     arm asks only about types/lib/path/no-default-lib — so `foo="bar"` gets
    ;     TS1084 either way. It still has to be PARSED, because the loop only
    ;     stops on a name that is empty or on a missing `=` or quote.
    ;
    ; ★★ The pragma scan reads the LEADING comment ranges of position 0, so it
    ; stops at the first thing that is neither whitespace nor a comment: a
    ; `/// <reference …>` below the first statement is not a pragma at all.
    ;
    ; ★ The comments arrive in SOURCE ORDER, which is what makes CheckJsDirective's
    ; last-wins rule an unconditional overwrite here — see extract_pragmas.
    procedure process_pragmas(mutable this, mutable file: ref[AstNode]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true linked


































    ; a directive carrying `preserve="true"` is kept for the declaration emitter
    ; (slice 250): kind 0 path, 1 types, 2 lib
    procedure record_reference_range(mutable this, kind: int, args: ref[Array[int]], id: int) @deep true @deep_this_4 true linked












    procedure record_preserved_reference(mutable this, args: ref[Array[int]], kind: int, id: int, mode: int) @persists true @deep true @deep_beyond_2 true linked














    function resolution_mode_value(this, value_pos: int, value_end: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked








    ; extractPragmas' single-line arm, and then the `reference` arm of
    ; processPragmasIntoFields on what it found. The reference keeps the two
    ; apart because it collects every pragma of the file before processing any;
    ; here each comment yields at most one pragma and the comments arrive in
    ; order, so processing per comment leaves the diagnostics in the same
    ; sequence. The one behaviour that split would buy is CheckJsDirective's
    ; "last of check/nocheck wins", which nothing here reads.
    ;
    ; ★ `pos += 10` rather than `pos += len(tagName) + 1` is the reference's own
    ; line, and it is safe for the same reason there: the branch is only reached
    ; when the name IS `reference`, i.e. when nine characters follow the `<`.
    procedure extract_pragmas(mutable this, comment_pos: int, comment_end: int) @deep true linked



























































































    ; processPragmasIntoFields' `reference` arm. Its four cases are ORDERED and
    ; the order is load-bearing: `no-default-lib="true"` swallows the directive
    ; whatever else it carries, and `types` beats `lib` beats `path`. Only the
    ; last case and the resolution-mode parse produce anything visible here.
    procedure process_reference_pragma(mutable this, comment_pos: int, comment_end: int, args: ref[Array[int]]) @deep true @deep_beyond_8 true @deep_this_8 true linked



















































    ; extractPragmas' MULTI-LINE arm (slice 119), and the header of this block used
    ; to say it was not ported.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE COMMENT IT REPLACES NAMED A READER AND THE READER WAS TOO NARROW. It
    ; read *"its only readers are the emitter's JSX factory selection and the
    ; checker's jsxImportSource, so porting it would be forty lines nothing in this
    ; repository can measure"* — and `markJsxAliasReferenced` is a third reader that
    ; REPORTS: it resolves the factory name as a USE, and when that name is an import
    ; alias whose module cannot be found, resolving it emits TS2307 a SECOND time.
    ; 17 stage-2 units carry that shape; 0 stage-1 units do, which is why this
    ; arrived with a stage-2 confirmation and not with a yardstick.
    ;
    ; ★★★ ONLY THE FIRST `@` ON A LINE COUNTS, and that is the whole of why
    ; `jsxPragmaAfterTokenSameLine` is not a pragma: the reference's regex is
    ; `/@(\S+)(\s+(?:\S.*)?)?$/gm`, so `Authored by foo@example.com @jsx h` yields the
    ; pragma name `example.com` and the scan then jumps to the end of the line. The
    ; loop below reproduces that by advancing to `line_end` after every candidate,
    ; whether or not it matched.
    ;
    ; ★ The four names the reference accepts are transcribed whole; only `jsx` and
    ; `jsxfrag` have a reader here, and the other two are matched so that a later
    ; slice adding one finds the arm rather than a hole.
    procedure extract_multi_line_pragmas(mutable this, comment_pos: int, comment_end: int) @deep true linked




















































































    ; `skipTo(text, pos, "@")`, and the three scanners the multi-line arm needs
    ; beside the two the single-line arm already had.
    function pragma_skip_to_at(this, pos: int, limit: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked











    function pragma_skip_non_blanks(this, pos: int, limit: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked












    function pragma_line_end(this, pos: int, limit: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked














    ; pragma_name_is, case-INSENSITIVELY: the reference lowercases the name before
    ; comparing, and `@jsxFrag` is how every test in the corpus spells it.
    function pragma_name_is_ci(this, pos: int, name_end: int, lit: Slice[char]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked
















    ; parseResolutionMode. The RESULT — a module kind — has no reader in this
    ; port; the diagnostic is the whole of what is left.
    procedure parse_resolution_mode(mutable this, value_pos: int, value_end: int) @deep true linked








    ; `strings.HasPrefix(text[pos:], s)`. Past the end is false rather than a
    ; panic, which is the one place Go's slice would have raised instead.
    function pragma_match(this, pos: int, limit: int, lit: Slice[char]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked













    function pragma_skip_blanks(this, pos: int, limit: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked












    ; extractName — letters and `-`, and the reference lowercases the result, so
    ; every comparison against it below is case-insensitive.
    function pragma_extract_name(this, pos: int, limit: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked



















    ; extractQuotedString. Answers the position of the CLOSING quote, or -1 when
    ; there is no opening quote or the string is never closed — the second is
    ; what `path="missingquote.ts />` is, and it is why that line ends up with an
    ; empty argument map and TS1084.
    function pragma_extract_quoted_string(this, pos: int, limit: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked
















    ; A name in the source, compared against an ASCII literal case-INSENSITIVELY
    ; (the reference lowercases the name it extracted).
    function pragma_name_is(this, pos: int, end: int, lit: Slice[char]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked
















    ; A VALUE in the source, compared exactly — a quoted string is not a name and
    ; the reference does not fold its case.
    function pragma_text_is(this, pos: int, end: int, lit: Slice[char]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked













    function pragma_arg_id(this, pos: int, end: int) @deep true returns int linked
















    ; The index of an argument's triple, or -1.
    function pragma_arg_find(this, args: ref[Array[int]], id: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns int linked












    ; args[name] = value — an existing entry is replaced, because the store is a
    ; map and `<reference path="a" path="b" />` names one file.
    procedure pragma_arg_set(this, mutable args: ref[Array[int]], id: int, value_pos: int, value_end: int) @page true @direct true @deep true linked













    function pragma_arg_value_is(this, args: ref[Array[int]], id: int, lit: Slice[char]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked







    ; ── the JSDoc token helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────

    procedure next_token_jsdoc(mutable this) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns int linked





    procedure next_jsdoc_comment_text_token(mutable this, in_backticks: bool) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns int linked





    procedure parse_expected_jsdoc(mutable this, kind: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked













    procedure parse_optional_jsdoc(mutable this, t: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked









    ; ★★★ THE ORDINARY parse_token_node, NOT A JSDOC ONE — and the reference has
    ; no JSDoc variant of it at all. That is not an oversight there and it is the
    ; only place in this grammar where the ORDINARY scanner is deliberately used
    ; to leave a JSDoc token: `p.parseTokenNode()` advances with `nextToken()`,
    ; which SKIPS TRIVIA, so the token after a consumed backtick has its full
    ; start at the whitespace rather than past it — and that full start is what
    ; the following tag comment begins at.
    ;
    ; Measured, on `` @param `d` markdown-quoted ``: with a JSDoc-scanning
    ; variant the whitespace becomes a token of its own, skip_whitespace_or_asterisk
    ; consumes it, and the JSDocText starts ONE BYTE LATE. It is the only
    ; difference the whole fixture produces, which is why it needed a fixture —
    ; no corpus unit has a backquoted @param name.
    procedure parse_optional_token_jsdoc(mutable this, kind: int) @page true @returnpage true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked






    procedure parse_expected_token_jsdoc(mutable this, kind: int) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked








    ; ── the type grammar inside a comment ───────────────────────────────────

    ; parseJSDocType. The ORDINARY type parser runs here — that is the point of
    ; the routine — with two things around it: the scanner is told to eat a
    ; leading `*` per line, so a type may be written across the comment's
    ; margin, and a trailing `=` makes the whole thing OPTIONAL, which is the
    ; Closure spelling `{string=}`.
    procedure parse_jsdoc_type(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked

















    ; parseJSDocAllType — the bare `*`, which is Closure's "any".
    procedure parse_jsdoc_all_type(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked






    ; parseJSDocTypeExpression. `mayOmitBraces` is true for the two tags whose
    ; type may be written bare (`@type string`, `@this Foo`) and false
    ; everywhere else, where a missing `{` is reported.
    ;
    ; ★ The JSDoc CONTEXT FLAG is set around the type and not around the whole
    ; comment: it is what makes `?` and `!` prefix operators and what
    ; parse_non_array_type tests. finish_node stamps it onto every node built
    ; inside, which is how the flag reaches the yardstick.
    procedure parse_jsdoc_type_expression(mutable this, may_omit_braces: bool) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked
















    ; parseJSDocNameReference — `@see {Foo.bar}`. The ResetPos is the reference's
    ; and is load-bearing: the name was read with the ORDINARY scanner (through
    ; parse_jsdoc_link_name's parse_identifier_name), so the scan has to be put
    ; back to the token's full start and re-read as a JSDoc token before the
    ; caller's comment loop continues.
    procedure parse_jsdoc_name_reference(mutable this) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked











    ; ── the comment ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; parseJSDocComment. The SAVE-AND-RESTORE around it is the whole of the
    ; routine's outer half, and every field it touches is one the inner parse
    ; will disturb: the scanner's END (truncated to just before `*/`), its whole
    ; state, the token, the context flags, the parsing contexts, and the
    ; diagnostics accumulated inside.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE DIAGNOSTICS ARE DISCARDED for a TS file and MOVED to a separate
    ; list for a JS one. That is not a shortcut: the reference does exactly this,
    ; and it is why the third yardstick compares SPANS and not diagnostics — a
    ; JSDoc parse error has no observable code anywhere the port can compare it.
    ; This port drops them rather than keeping a second list, and says so here:
    ; the list would have no reader, and its reader arrives with the checker.
    procedure parse_jsdoc_comment(mutable this, parent: ref[AstNode]?, start: int, comment_end: int, full_start: int) @page true @returnpage true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked






































































    ; parseJSDocCommentWorker — the state machine that turns a comment into a
    ; JSDoc node: prose becomes JSDocText, `{@link X}` becomes a node of its own,
    ; and an `@` at the start of a line hands over to parse_tag.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `state` VARIABLE IS THE GRAMMAR. BeginningOfLine admits a tag;
    ; SawAsterisk is what a margin `*` leaves behind and still admits one;
    ; SavingComments and SavingBackticks do not, and they also switch the
    ; SCANNER — a comment run is read by scan_jsdoc_comment_text_token and
    ; everything else by scan_jsdoc_token. The reference PANICS if whitespace
    ; arrives while saving, because the text scanner would have eaten it.
    procedure parse_jsdoc_comment_worker(mutable this, start: int, comment_end: int, full_start: int, initial_indent: int) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked






















































































































































































































































    ; ── whitespace skipping ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; isNextNonwhitespaceTokenEndOfFile. INFINITE lookahead, as the reference's
    ; own comment says: there can be any number of newlines before the end.
    procedure is_next_nonwhitespace_token_end_of_file(mutable this) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked















    procedure look_ahead_is_next_nonwhitespace_token_end_of_file(mutable this) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked







    ; skipWhitespace. The look-ahead is what keeps the whitespace before the end
    ; of a comment OUT of every node's range — a node that ended at the `*/`
    ; would be a different tree.
    procedure skip_whitespace(mutable this) @deep true linked










    ; skipWhitespaceOrAsterisk. Answers the INDENT TEXT — the run of whitespace
    ; and margin asterisks after the LAST line break — as a source range, or an
    ; empty one when no line break was crossed.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference builds it with `strings.Join(indents, "")` over the tokens
    ; it collected, and those tokens are CONSECUTIVE (nothing is trivia inside a
    ; JSDoc comment), so the join is exactly the source span from the first kept
    ; token to the last. That is what makes a pair of offsets faithful here and
    ; not an approximation.
    procedure skip_whitespace_or_asterisk(mutable this, mutable out_start: ref[int], mutable out_end: ref[int]) @deep true linked

























































    ; ── the tag dispatcher ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; parseTag. The reference's `switch tagName.Text()`, and the arms are
    ; independent `if`s that return for the reason mapping decision (2) gives:
    ; an `else if` chain nests one planner level per arm.
    ;
    ; ★ The six SIMPLE tags take a KIND where the reference passes a factory
    ; CLOSURE (§3.5e): every call site is static, so the func value carries no
    ; information the kind does not.
    procedure parse_tag(mutable this, tags: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?, margin: int) @page true @returnpage true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked


































































    ; parseTrailingTagComments. `margin += end - pos` only when there is NO
    ; indent text, which is the reference's way of saying "the tag's own text
    ; counts towards the margin when the tag did not start a fresh line".
    procedure parse_trailing_tag_comments(mutable this, pos: int, tag_end: int, margin: int, it_start: int, it_end: int) @page true @returnpage true @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]? linked

















    ; parseTagComments — the same state machine as the comment worker, over the
    ; text that FOLLOWS a tag. It differs in three places and each one is a real
    ; difference rather than a duplication: an `@` here ENDS the comment (and the
    ; scanner is reset so the caller sees the `@` again), the newline arm does
    ; not go through pushComment so the margin survives a line break, and a
    ; leading margin asterisk starts recording on the NEXT token rather than
    ; immediately.
    procedure parse_tag_comments(mutable this, initial_indent: int, has_initial_margin: bool, im_start: int, im_end: int) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]? linked



























































































































































































































    ; ── inline links ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; parseJSDocLink — `{@link Foo}`, `{@linkcode Foo}`, `{@linkplain Foo}`.
    ; Answers null when the brace did not open one, and the caller then treats
