    procedure parse_template_tag(mutable this, start: int, tag_name: ref[AstNode]?, indent: int, it_start: int, it_end: int) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked









    ; ── names ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; parseJSDocEntityName. `y[]` is accepted as an entity name and the brackets
    ; are dropped — the reference's own comment says it is not worth enforcing
    ; the Closure rule that would require them.
    procedure parse_jsdoc_entity_name(mutable this, code: int) @page true @returnpage true @deep true returns ref[AstNode] linked















    ; parseJSDocIdentifierName. `code = 0` is the reference's `nil` message, and
    ; then a RESERVED WORD gets its own report instead — which is the one place
    ; the two differ.
    procedure parse_jsdoc_identifier_name(mutable this, code: int) @page true @direct true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked





















    ; ── the JSDoc REPARSER (slice 22) ───────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; internal/parser/reparser.go, and it is what `.js` means. For a JavaScript
    ; file JSDoc is not a comment that documents the code — it IS the type
    ; syntax, so `@typedef` has to become a type alias in the statement list and
    ; `@param {T} x` has to put a type annotation on a parameter that has none.
    ; The reference does that in one pass, hanging off with_jsdoc, and splits it
    ; in two:
    ;
    ;   reparse_unhosted   a tag that becomes a NEW declaration (@typedef,
    ;                      @callback, @import, @overload) — it goes into
    ;                      `reparse_list` and parse_list flushes it into the
    ;                      enclosing statement list, BEFORE the statement the
    ;                      comment documented.
    ;   reparse_hosted     a tag that MUTATES the node it documents (@type,
    ;                      @satisfies, @template, @param, @this, @return, the
    ;                      five modifier tags, @implements, @augments).
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE MUTATING HALF IS WHY THE AST GREW SLOTS THIS SLICE. Nothing the
    ; grammar can write fills FunctionDeclaration.full_signature,
    ; BinaryExpression.type_node, PropertyAssignment.type_node,
    ; ShorthandPropertyAssignment.type_node or ExportAssignment.type_node — every
    ; one of them exists for a tag, and every earlier slice's record comment said
    ; so and named the place in the child order. They are filled here.
    ;
    ; ★★★ CLONING: the reference deep-clones every node it lifts out of the JSDoc
    ; tree into the statement tree (`DeepCloneReparse`), and SINCE SLICE 144 SO
    ; DOES THIS PORT. Slices 22–27 copied the ROOT and shared the children on
    ; §3.5bf's argument; see add_deep_clone_reparse for what expired it.
    ;
    ; ★ The reference additionally keeps `reparsedClones`, sorted, on the
    ; SourceFile, for the binder's benefit (a symbol is attached to the reparsed
    ; node, not to the JSDoc node). Nothing here reads it and neither yardstick
    ; can see it, so it is not carried — stated rather than silently dropped.

    ; finishReparsedNode. The flags are ASSIGNED, not OR'd: a synthesized node
    ; carries the context it was made in plus Reparsed and nothing else.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE THIRD LINE — overrideParentInImmediateChildren — WAS MISSING UNTIL
    ; SLICE 50, AND NO DUMP COULD SEE IT. `parent` is not in the token stream, not
    ; in the tree walk (which compares depth, kind, pos, end and flags) and not on a
    ; symbol, so a synthesized node whose children still name the JSDoc node they
    ; were parsed under compared exactly equal on all four earlier yardsticks. The
    ; reader that found it is the CHECKER: `@typedef {T} A.B.MyType` builds nested
    ; ModuleDeclarations, checkGrammarModuleElementContext asks the inner one for
    ; its parent's kind, and with the line missing the answer was neither
    ; SourceFile nor ModuleBlock nor ModuleDeclaration — so the port INVENTED a
    ; TS1235 the reference does not report, on one unit of the stage-1 corpus.
    ;
    ; ★★ Re-parenting steals nothing here, and since slice 144 the argument is the
    ; short one: the immediate children of a reparsed node are freshly SYNTHESIZED
    ; or the root of an add_deep_clone_reparse, and a clone now OWNS its whole
    ; subtree. The long form this note used to carry — §3.5bf's claim that a
    ; shared child's parent answers every question the same — is retired with the
    ; shortcut it defended.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SIBLING `finishMutatedNode` IS ANSWERED NOW (slice 78), AND THE NOTE
    ; THAT STOOD HERE PREDICTED ITS OWN INSTRUMENT. It read *it is a marker rather
    ; than a gap, and the instrument that would show it is the one that showed this:
    ; a checker arm reading a parent* — and that arm arrived the moment slice 78
    ; tightened the checker's JSDoc gate, because a node the reparser mutates is by
    ; construction a node carrying a doc comment, i.e. exactly the population the
    ; old gate refused to check. Three units invented a diagnostic on the first run:
    ; TS2680 twice (`parameter_index_of` searching the WRONG function's parameter
    ; list — the synthesized `this` param's `parent` still named nothing) and TS1277
    ; once (checkGrammarModifiers asking a reparsed type parameter's parent whether
    ; it is function-like). ★★★**A stale parent is not a wrong answer, it is a
    ; DIFFERENT QUESTION**: both reports are correct about the tree they were shown.
    ; ★And the old note's fear was right too and is what bounds the fix — see
    ; finish_mutated_node.
    procedure finish_reparsed_node(this, mutable n: ref[AstNode]?, location: ref[AstNode]?) @deep true linked











    ; ★★★ finishMutatedNode — `overrideParentInImmediateChildren` and nothing else,
    ; at the twenty sites of reparser.go where a node's children are REPLACED IN
    ; PLACE rather than built by finish_node (slice 78).
    ;
    ; ★★★ IT RE-PARENTS THE MUTATED NODE'S OWN IMMEDIATE CHILDREN, every one of
    ; which genuinely belongs to it — the new type, the new parameter list, the
    ; cast that now wraps the old expression.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE DISTINCTION THIS NOTE USED TO DRAW AGAINST THE DEEP CLONE IS
    ; GONE (slice 144). It read *one level down is the whole difference, and it is
    ; why the reference can call the same loop from both places and this port
    ; cannot* — true only while the clone SHARED its children, where re-parenting
    ; would have taken a child's parent away from a node still in the JSDoc tree.
    ; The clone now copies the children, so this port calls a parent setter from
    ; both places exactly as the reference does; the clone's is the RECURSIVE one,
    ; because a clone owns its whole subtree and a mutated node owns one level.
    ; ★Control g78/g17 measured the old steal as invisible to all three
    ; instruments, which is why the divergence could stand for a hundred slices
    ; and why the reader that ended it was a fourth one — see the clone.
    ;
    ; ★ Kept as its own name rather than calling the loop directly at twenty sites:
    ; the reference has the name, and the sites are then greppable against it.
    procedure finish_mutated_node(this, mutable n: ref[AstNode]?) @deep true linked






    ; DeepCloneReparse — the visitor, then SetParentInChildren, then the flag, in
    ; the reference's own order (ast/deepclone.go).
    ;
    ; ★★★ WHAT EXPIRED HERE, AND IT WAS NOT A WRITE. Slices 22–27 copied the ROOT
    ; and SHARED the children, and the argument (§3.5bf) was exact about what
    ; would end it: *a mutation reaching a SHARED child*, whose symptom would be a
    ; JSDoc-yardstick failure on a unit whose parser yardstick passed. That
    ; mutation never arrived. The reader that arrived instead only READS: a scope
    ; chain IS the parent chain, and a shared child's parent still names the node
    ; the JSDoc tree owns, so `@template T` + `@typedef {T} Alias` resolved `T`
    ; from a chain that walks up the JSDoc comment and never reaches the
    ; SourceFile — where upstream's `T` sits under the alias. The checker stopped
    ; on it at 19 units of the stage-1 corpus (`jsdoc-reparse-shared-parent`, the
    ; frontier's largest row). ★★★A structural device that separates two ALIASES
    ; is skippable while nothing WRITES through either of them — and reading a
    ; parent is not a write. That is the half §3.5bf's general form did not say.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE ARM SET IS BOUNDED AND THE FALLBACK IS THE OLD BEHAVIOUR. A LEAF
    ; needs no arm at all — `set c: *src` copies the payload by value, and a node
    ; with no child slot is thereby deep-cloned — so only a kind WITH children
    ; needs one, and a kind without an arm falls back to exactly the root copy
    ; that stood here before. That fallback is conservative in the one direction
    ; that matters: its shared child's chain still fails to reach the file, so the
    ; checker's own test REPORTS the unit rather than answering it out of the
    ; wrong tree. The arms below are the kinds a reparse can lift — measured over
    ; the stage-1 corpus as 21 clone-root kinds and 32 kinds inside clone
    ; subtrees, and written out as every type node, the entity names, the
    ; signature members, the import forms and the two function-like declarations.
    procedure add_deep_clone_reparse(mutable this, mutable n: ref[AstNode]?) @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked









    ; The visitor's nil arm. Every child slot below is asked through this one, so
    ; the recursion needs no null test of its own.
    procedure deep_clone(mutable this, n: ref[AstNode]?) @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked






