    ; "name", descriptor)` — three arguments, the callee spelled out as a
    ; property access of the identifier `Object`, and a literal property name.
    ; The FIRST argument decides between the two ObjectDefineProperty kinds and
    ; is read by the caller.
    function is_bindable_object_define_property_call(n: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked
































    ; ast.GetAssignmentDeclarationKind, whole. Two arms; `None` for everything
    ; else, including every assignment that is not one of these five shapes.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE `Property` ARM IS OUTSIDE THE JS TEST, and that one line is why
    ; this classifier belongs to the binder rather than to the JSDoc reparser:
    ; `f.x = 1` in a TypeScript file is an expando declaration upstream, so a
    ; port that answered `None` for every TS file would silently claim agreement
    ; on exactly the units where the reference declares an extra symbol.
    function get_assignment_declaration_kind(n: ref[AstNode]?) @deep true returns int linked

































































    ; ast.IsRequireCall. The reference's second parameter asks whether the one
    ; argument must be a string literal; both callers this port has pass FALSE,
    ; and the parameter is kept rather than folded away because the reference's
    ; other callers pass TRUE and §3.5ar's lesson is that a signature narrowed to
    ; today's callers costs more the second time.
    function is_require_call(n: ref[AstNode]?, require_string_literal_like_argument: bool) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked






















    ; ast.IsImportCall — slice 72, and ONE spelling since slice 138.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE SECOND DISJUNCT IS WHY THIS IS A FUNCTION AND NOT AN INLINE KIND
    ; TEST. checkExpressionWorker's CallExpression arm asks it to choose between
    ; checkImportCallExpression and checkCallExpression, and slice 70 transcribed
    ; only the first half there — `Checker.kind_or_unknown(expression_of(node)) =
    ; KindImportKeyword` — with the note *"which is a kind test and needs nothing
    ; else"*. That is true of `import("m")` and false of `import.defer("m")`, whose
    ; callee is a MetaProperty: the reference sends BOTH to
    ; checkImportCallExpression and the port sent the second to
    ; check_call_expression.
    ;
    ; ★★★ THE BODY MOVED TO ast.scaly, where the CHECKER's own reader already
    ; looked. This copy carried the `import.defer` arm and `AstNode.is_import_call`
    ; did not, so the two disagreed for as long as both existed; see the header
    ; there for what that cost.
    function is_import_call(n: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked


    ; ast.IsConstTypeReference — `x as const`. Three tests, and the LAST one is the
    ; reason `as const` is not a keyword: `const` in type position is an ordinary
    ; type reference whose name happens to be the word.
    function is_const_type_reference(n: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked
















    ; HasSamePropertyAccessName.
    function has_same_property_access_name(a: ref[AstNode]?, b: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked































    ; findMatchingParameter: the tag's own index among the comment's @param tags
    ; decides the match when the names do not — which is what makes
    ; `@param {T}` with no name at all reach the right parameter.
    procedure find_matching_parameter(this, fun: ref[AstNode], parameter_tag: ref[AstNode], jsdoc: ref[AstNode]?) @page true @direct true @ret_param_1 true @deep true returns ref[AstNode]? linked






























































    ; ── reparseTags and the two halves ──────────────────────────────────────

    procedure reparse_tags(mutable this, mutable parent: ref[AstNode], mutable jsdoc: ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]?) @deep true @deep_this_4 true linked


























    ; A tag that becomes a declaration of its own.
    procedure reparse_unhosted(mutable this, mutable tag: ref[AstNode], mutable parent: ref[AstNode], mutable jsdoc: ref[AstNode]?) @deep true @deep_this_8 true @deep_whole_8 true linked































































































    ; A tag that mutates the node it documents.
    procedure reparse_hosted(mutable this, mutable tag: ref[AstNode], mutable parent: ref[AstNode], mutable jsdoc: ref[AstNode]?) @deep true linked












































































































































































    ; The @type tag's HOSTED arms — the switch over the documented node's kind.
    ; Answers whether the tag was consumed, so the caller can fall through to the
    ; function-like host the reference falls through to.
    procedure reparse_type_tag_on_host(mutable this, mutable tag: ref[AstNode], mutable parent: ref[AstNode]) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked






















































































    procedure reparse_satisfies_tag(mutable this, mutable tag: ref[AstNode], mutable parent: ref[AstNode]) @deep true linked























































































    ; @readonly / @private / @public / @protected / @override become real
    ; modifiers on the member they document.
    function is_jsdoc_modifier_tag(k: int) @ret_nonlocal true @deep true returns bool linked












    procedure reparse_modifier_tag(mutable this, tag: ref[AstNode], mutable parent_in: ref[AstNode]) @deep true linked






































































    procedure reparse_implements_tag(mutable this, mutable tag: ref[AstNode], mutable parent: ref[AstNode]) @deep true linked












































    ; @augments {Base<T>} fills in the type arguments of an `extends Base` the
    ; source wrote without any — and only when the two name the same base.
    procedure reparse_augments_tag(mutable this, mutable tag: ref[AstNode], mutable parent: ref[AstNode]) @deep true linked












































    ; ── the entry points ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    ; parseJSDocForNode — the LAZY parse a TS/TSX file defers to. The reference
    ; runs it on a fresh parser out of a pool and throws its diagnostics away;
    ; here it runs on the parser that produced the tree, which is the same thing
    ; with one region instead of a pool, and parse_jsdoc_comment already restores
    ; every field it touches.
    procedure parse_jsdoc_for_node(mutable this, mutable n: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]? linked






















































    ; initializeState's context-flag switch, as a function, because the lazy
    ; JSDoc accessor above needs the value a FRESH parser would start from.
    function initial_context_flags(this) @deep true returns int linked










    ; The same walk without the flag gate. with_jsdoc needs it for a JavaScript
    ; file, where the flag is set only AFTER a comment has parsed — the gate above
    ; belongs to the ACCESSOR, which is asked about a node whose parse is over.
    procedure parse_jsdoc_comments_of(mutable this, mutable n: ref[AstNode]?) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]? linked





































    ; The program-facing form. A concept STATIC taking the parser explicitly,
    ; because a user program calling a cross-package method on a concept pointer
    ; does not resolve while a static does (root CLAUDE.md).
    ; under enter_shared (slice 365): a lazy parse writes the file's parser and its page
    procedure jsdoc_for_node(mutable p: ref[Parser], mutable n: ref[AstNode]?) @page true @direct true @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]? reads shared_lock linked







    ; ★★★ Node.EagerJSDoc(file), slice 78. `JSDoc(file)` falls through to
    ; `resolveJSDoc(node)` for a lazy file, i.e. it PARSES the comment on demand;
    ; EagerJSDoc reads the cache and stops, *"without triggering lazy JSDoc
    ; parsing"* in the reference's own comment. checkSourceElementWorker is the only
    ; caller in checker.go and it asks the eager form, so for a TS or TSX file the
    ; checker's JSDoc pass sees only what with_jsdoc parsed eagerly — a comment
    ; carrying `@see` or `@link` — and nothing for any other doc comment. For a
    ; `.js` file there is no deferral and the cache holds everything.
    ;
    ; ★★★ AND THE TWO ANSWER THE SAME THING TO THE ONLY QUESTION THIS PORT'S CALLER
    ; ASKS — measured, and it refutes what this note said when it was written.
    ; Control g78/g02 swaps in `jsdoc_for_node` and NOTHING MOVES on any of three
    ; instruments. The reason is a coincidence of two conditions and it is a proof
    ; rather than a corpus fact: `with_jsdoc` parses eagerly exactly when the comment
    ; carries `@see` or `@link`, and `check_jsdoc_comment` reacts to exactly one
    ; thing, a LINK part. So a comment holding a link is always in the cache, and a
    ; comment absent from the cache holds no link — the lazy parse can only ever
    ; produce parts the pass then ignores.
    ;
    ; ★★ It is still this function that is called, for three reasons that survive
    ; the coincidence: it is the reference's own call at that line, it does no work
    ; where the lazy form parses a comment per node, and the coincidence is a
    ; property of `jsdoc_scanner_info`'s TRIGGER SET — widen that set and the two
    ; stop agreeing, with nothing to notice it.
    ;
    ; ★ The flag test is EagerJSDoc's own first line and control g03 shows it is
    ; currently redundant in both directions — a JS file sets the flag in the same
    ; branch that fills the table, and a TS file's untouched cache answers null
    ; anyway. Transcribed so a reader comparing the two functions finds the same
    ; two tests.
    function eager_jsdoc(p: ref[Parser], n: ref[AstNode]) @ret_param_0 true @deep true returns ref[Array[ref[AstNode]?]]? linked





}
